Let $E$ denote the given expression. Apply $\sin x\cos x=\frac{1}{2}\sin(2x)$ at $x=56.25^\circ$. Dividing by secant multiplies by cosine because $1/\sec x=\cos x$. The resulting pair at $x=112.5^\circ$ permits a second application.
$$
\begin{aligned}
E&=\frac{2\,\sin\left(112.5^\circ\right)}{\sec\left(112.5^\circ\right)}\\&=2\,\sin\left(112.5^\circ\right)\cos\left(112.5^\circ\right)\\&=\sin\left(225^\circ\right)
\end{aligned}
$$
The terminal sine is $\sin\left(225^\circ\right)=- \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}$.
Therefore,
$$
\begin{aligned}
E&=\left(- \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\right)\\&=- \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Let $E$ denote the given expression. Apply $\sin x\cos x=\frac{1}{2}\sin(2x)$ at $x=11.25^\circ$ in the denominator. Use $\sec x=1/\cos x$, then apply the double-angle identity again at $x=22.5^\circ$.
$$
\begin{aligned}
E&=\frac{4\,\sec\left(22.5^\circ\right)}{\sin\left(22.5^\circ\right)}\\&=\frac{4}{\sin\left(22.5^\circ\right)\cos\left(22.5^\circ\right)}\\&=\frac{8}{\sin\left(45^\circ\right)}
\end{aligned}
$$
The terminal sine is $\sin\left(45^\circ\right)=\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}$.
Rationalize the denominator by multiplying numerator and denominator by $\sqrt{2}$:
$$
\begin{aligned}
E&=\frac{8}{\left(\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\right)}\\&=\frac{16}{\sqrt{2}}\\&=8 \sqrt{2}.
\end{aligned}
$$

